{=============================================================================
  Elementary steam power plant (Rankine cycle, component balances)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0053
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Steady-flow analysis of an elementary steam power plant, component by
  component (pump, economiser, steam generator, turbine, condenser), each one
  an open system at steady state with one inlet and one outlet. Mass balance
  q_m_out = q_m_in = q_m; first law Q_dot + W_dot = q_m*(Delta h + Delta EC +
  Delta EP) with the kinetic-energy term kept where the velocities are
  significant (turbine exhaust, main steam line). Given the state points 1-7
  with pressure drops between components and a thermal loss between 4-5, the
  model computes the turbine power, the heat rates of the condenser,
  economiser and steam generator, the cooling-water flow rate (temperature
  rise 15 -> 25 °C) and the overall cycle efficiency. As in the original
  exercise (statement "Exercise 6", based on a textbook exercise 5.51).

  Inputs : pressures and temperatures of states 1-7, turbine exhaust quality
           and velocity, steam mass flow rate, pump power, pipe diameters,
           cooling-water temperature rise
  Outputs: W_dot_turb, Q_dot_cond, Q_dot_econ, Q_dot_gen, q_m_rfd,
           eta_rankine, state-point arrays p[i], h[i], T[i], s[i], v[i]
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiège course Thermodynamique appliquée (MECA0002), exercise
           session R3 2022-2023, exercise 6 - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"Elementary steam power plant. Compute:
 a) the power output of the turbine
 b) the heat transfer rate in the condenser, the economiser and the steam generator
 c) the cooling-water flow rate in the condenser, if the cooling-water
    temperature rises there from 15 to 25 °C"

"Data (original values in kPa, kW)"
p_1 = 6200E3  [Pa]   "6200 kPa in the original"
p_2 = 6100E3  [Pa]   "6100 kPa in the original"
T_2 = 45  [C]
p_3 = 5900E3  [Pa]   "5900 kPa in the original"
T_3 = 175  [C]
p_4 = 5700E3  [Pa]   "5700 kPa in the original"
T_4 = 500  [C]
p_5 = 5500E3  [Pa]   "5500 kPa in the original"
T_5 = 490  [C]
p_6 = 10E3  [Pa]     "10 kPa in the original"
x_6 = 0.92  [-]
c_6 = 200 [m/s]
p_7 = 9E3  [Pa]      "9 kPa in the original"
T_7 = 40   [C]
q_m_vap = 25 [kg/s]
W_dot_pump = 300E3  [W]   "300 kW in the original"
d_45 = 0.2 [m]
d_7123 = 0.075 [m]
DeltaT_rfd = 10 [C]

"Remark: each component can be considered as an open system at steady state.
 All components have a single inlet and a single outlet, so the mass balance
 is q_m_out = q_m_in = q_m and the first law reads
 Q_dot + W_dot = q_m (Delta h + Delta EC + Delta EP).
 For each component the exercise consists in keeping the relevant terms."

"a) Power output of the turbine"
"Control volume around the turbine."
"The turbine is assumed adiabatic (very fast transformation, and no
 information in the statement about heat exchange with the surroundings)
 -> Q_dot = 0"
"The variation of potential energy is neglected with respect to the
 variation of enthalpy. The first law for the turbine becomes:"
W_dot_turb = q_m_vap * (h_6 - h_5 + c_6^2/2 - c_5^2/2)   "kinetic terms in J/kg as in the original (kJ/J factors removed with the unit conversion)"
h_6 = enthalpy(water,p=p_6,x=x_6)   "at the outlet the fluid is two-phase -> arguments P and x"
h_5 = enthalpy(water,T=T_5,p=p_5)   "at the inlet superheated steam -> arguments P and T"
"The velocity at station 5 follows from the mass flow rate and the pipe
 cross-section (pipe diameter):"
q_m_vap = rho_5*c_5*A_5
A_5 = pi*d_45^2/4
rho_5 = 1/volume(water,T=T_5,p=p_5)
"The power is negative from the viewpoint of the steam because it is given to
 the outside of the control volume -> the machine produces work (as in the original)"

"b) Heat transfer rate in the condenser, the economiser and the steam generator"

"b.1) Condenser"
"Control volume around the condenser."
"No work transfer in the condenser -> W_dot = 0"
"The variation of potential energy is neglected."
Q_dot_cond = q_m_vap*(h_7-h_6 + c_7^2/2 - c_6^2/2)
h_7 = enthalpy(water,p=p_7,T=T_7)
q_m_vap = rho_7*c_7*A_7
A_7 = pi*d_7123^2/4
rho_7 = 1/volume(water,T=T_7,p=p_7)
"The heat rate in the condenser is NEGATIVE from the viewpoint of the working
 fluid because the vapour-to-liquid phase change is exothermic (as in the original)"

"b.2) Economiser"
"Control volume around the economiser."
"No work transfer in the economiser -> W_dot = 0"
"The variation of potential energy is neglected."
Q_dot_econ = q_m_vap*(h_3-h_2 + c_3^2/2 - c_2^2/2)
h_3 = enthalpy(water,p=p_3,T=T_3)
h_2 = enthalpy(water,p=p_2,T=T_2)
q_m_vap = rho_3*c_3*A_3
A_3 = A_7
rho_3 = 1/volume(water,T=T_3,p=p_3)
q_m_vap = rho_2*c_2*A_2
A_2 = A_7
rho_2 = 1/volume(water,T=T_2,p=p_2)

"The heat rate at the economiser is POSITIVE from the viewpoint of the
 working fluid, which receives heat there (as in the original)."

"b.3) Steam generator"
"Control volume around the steam generator."
"No work transfer in the steam generator -> W_dot = 0"
"The variation of potential energy is neglected."
Q_dot_gen = q_m_vap*(h_4-h_3 + c_4^2/2 - c_3^2/2)
h_4 = enthalpy(water,p=p_4,T=T_4)
q_m_vap = rho_4*c_4*A_4
A_4 = A_5
rho_4 = 1/volume(water,P=p_4,T=T_4)
"The heat rate is again positive from the viewpoint of the working fluid,
 which receives it (as in the original)"

"c) Required cooling-water flow rate"
"The cooling circuit dissipates the heat extracted from the working fluid in
 the condenser. Power absorbed by the cooling system:"
Q_dot_rfd = q_m_rfd*C_eau*DeltaT_rfd
C_eau = 4184  [J/kg-K]   "4.184 kJ/kg-K in the original (liquid water)"
C_eau_EES = specheat(Water,T=20,x=0)   "value computed by EES for comparison, as in the original"
Q_dot_rfd = abs(Q_dot_cond)

"Note: the velocities at stations 2, 3 and 7 come out below 10 m/s -> their
 contribution could be neglected in the component balances without a large
 error (as in the original)"

"Bonus: a global balance over the cycle should reveal the importance of the
 energy losses (thermal losses, pressure drops), as in the original."
W_dot_pump+Q_dot_econ+Q_dot_gen+W_dot_turb+Q_dot_cond+Pertes=0
"Pressure drops appear between 1-2, 2-3, 3-4, 4-5, 6-7 and a thermal loss
 between 4-5 (as in the original)"

"Overall efficiency = net useful power / power consumed"
eta_rankine=-(W_dot_turb+W_dot_pump)/(Q_dot_gen+Q_dot_econ)
"(sign correction in the numerator to obtain a positive efficiency)"

"Cycle on a diagram: array variables to store the state points in a table and
 display them on a property plot (as in the original)"
p[1]=p_1
p[2]=p_2
p[3]=p_3
p[4]=p_4
p[5]=p_5
p[6]=p_6
p[7]=p_7
h[1]=h_1
h[2]=h_2
h[3]=h_3
h[4]=h_4
h[5]=h_5
h[6]=h_6
h[7]=h_7
v[1]=1/rho_1
v[2]=1/rho_2
v[3]=1/rho_3
v[4]=1/rho_4
v[5]=1/rho_5
v[6]=1/rho_6
v[7]=1/rho_7
rho_1 = 1/volume(water,h=h_1,p=p_1)
rho_6 = 1/volume(water,h=h_6,p=p_6)
"pump outlet enthalpy h_1: first law applied to the pump, W = q_m*(h_1-h_7)"
W_dot_pump=q_m_vap*(h_1-h_7)

"State points for the thermodynamic diagrams (post-processing added for the
 CoolSolve library; the results above are unchanged)"
T[1]=temperature(Water,P=p[1],h=h[1])
T[2]=temperature(Water,P=p[2],h=h[2])
T[3]=temperature(Water,P=p[3],h=h[3])
T[4]=temperature(Water,P=p[4],h=h[4])
T[5]=temperature(Water,P=p[5],h=h[5])
T[6]=temperature(Water,P=p[6],h=h[6])
T[7]=temperature(Water,P=p[7],h=h[7])
s[1]=entropy(Water,P=p[1],h=h[1])
s[2]=entropy(Water,P=p[2],h=h[2])
s[3]=entropy(Water,P=p[3],h=h[3])
s[4]=entropy(Water,P=p[4],h=h[4])
s[5]=entropy(Water,P=p[5],h=h[5])
s[6]=entropy(Water,P=p[6],h=h[6])
s[7]=entropy(Water,P=p[7],h=h[7])
